] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566189 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7c32e90-669a-52ff-9868-695c63e890c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y-21$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52455c8c-f427-542e-9cf2-e8b407fdf74f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566227 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-218758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566232 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+32x+40$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fcb379b-2e62-54ea-ab65-5d0b75f0dbcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566240 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-12x+35)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71c17160-e343-5b78-8eee-e55ba4967a40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566242 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71daf213-45ae-576f-97ae-9ffbff2f758a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566243 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $7{x}^{2}-21x$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3f44aac-b56c-541f-849d-0822403501d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566256 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-8y+60)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb8a5938-134a-51c9-838d-3e4010a8ca50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566260 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-4{x}^{2}+8x$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566264 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-25252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-{a}^{2}-10a+16$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e52c4055-fdc4-580f-9736-b9940edca056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566268 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
